{"id":"far-above-classmates","title":"Paid furthest above their own classmates, overtime set aside","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"In large job classes, who is paid at least 40% more than the middle holder of the same class once overtime is left out?","finding":"Of 19,968 full-year holders of the 115 largest classes in fiscal 2026, 36 were paid at least 1.4 times the middle holder of their own class in salary and other pay together. For 27 the difference is mainly regular salary and for 9 mainly other pay; 24 have a regular salary more than a quarter above the top of the class’s published scale. The commonest classes are Assoc Engineer (7), Engineer (5), Registered Nurse (4) and EMT/Paramedic/Firefighter (4). Furthest above: Scott Neu, Deputy Sheriff (class 8304), Sheriff, $1.3 million against a class middle of $174,603.","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2026, the classes with 50 or more full-year holders. Pay is regular salary plus other pay, overtime left out, set against the middle value for the class citywide. “Mainly” says which of the two is further above the class’s own middle value. “Above the published scale” marks a regular salary more than 25% over the highest published step or range ceiling for a full-time year. Titles are printed as the payroll prints them (Dep is deputy, Chf is chief, Asst is assistant, Sprv is supervisor, Ofc is officer, EMT is emergency medical technician).","limits":"Several of the engineering and architecture names also appear, for the same reason, under City jobs (“Paid far above the top of the published scale”), where the likely explanation is set out: a higher-level assignment or second appointment recorded under the person’s permanent class. A single very high regular salary is usually back pay or a settlement paid in one year. High other pay can be a payout of leave taken in service, standby or retention pay. The middle of a class is pulled down by new entrants, so long service alone accounts for part of any gap. Nothing here says a payment was improper.","columns":[{"k":"person","t":"Name on the payroll","type":"person"},{"k":"job","t":"Job class","type":"text"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"salary","t":"Regular salary","type":"usd"},{"k":"other","t":"Other pay","type":"usd"},{"k":"pay","t":"Together","type":"usd"},{"k":"classMiddle","t":"Middle holder of the class","type":"usd"},{"k":"times","t":"Against the middle holder","type":"times"},{"k":"classmates","t":"Full-year holders","type":"int"},{"k":"why","t":"Mainly","type":"text"},{"k":"aboveScale","t":"Above the published scale","type":"text"},{"k":"overtime","t":"Overtime, not counted","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"person":"Scott Neu","job":"Deputy Sheriff (class 8304)","department":"Sheriff","salary":1138861,"other":132343,"pay":1271204,"classMiddle":174603,"times":7.28,"classmates":185,"overtime":217606,"why":"Regular salary","aboveScale":"Yes"},{"person":"Edmund Lee","job":"Junior Engineer (class 5201)","department":"Public Works","salary":286278,"other":0,"pay":286278,"classMiddle":128276,"times":2.23,"classmates":98,"overtime":0,"why":"Regular salary","aboveScale":"Yes"},{"person":"Brian Philpott","job":"Senior Management Assistant (class 1844)","department":"Police","salary":208042,"other":78128,"pay":286169,"classMiddle":139973,"times":2.04,"classmates":108,"overtime":15565,"why":"Other pay","aboveScale":"Yes"},{"person":"Jerome Manalastas","job":"Registered Nurse (class 2320)","department":"Public Health","salary":200420,"other":216328,"pay":416748,"classMiddle":221630,"times":1.88,"classmates":995,"overtime":38788,"why":"Other pay","aboveScale":"No"},{"person":"Paul Barradas","job":"Assistant Engineer (class 5203)","department":"Public Works","salary":286289,"other":0,"pay":286289,"classMiddle":158437,"times":1.81,"classmates":164,"overtime":0,"why":"Regular salary","aboveScale":"Yes"},{"person":"Jonathan Cantu","job":"Junior Engineer (class 5201)","department":"Public Utilities Commission","salary":23008